What I learned, however, is that healers seem to be easier than tourists
by a significant margin.

Archaeologists, on the other hand, seem to be almost as difficult as
tourists. Which is counter my intuition and experience. But my
statistical sample is small: I played all the vanilla classes until I
ascended one of them and repeated only very few after. I needed four
attempts to ascend an archaeologist, six to ascend a healer and seven to
ascend a tourist. (I think I needed four attempts for the rogue.) Seven
is my overall maximum, even if I add my slash'em games (where I do not
perform as well).

I don't know whether the protection racket significantly contributes to
your statistics. There was an argument going on several times that
healers fare best this way. Maybe it is true after all...

The speed of an archaeologist will "often" help him survive until he is
level 5. And then he can dip for Excalibur. With speed and Excalibur,
the probability to survive the next few dungeon levels increases a lot.

